@page "/swap"

<PageTitle>Swap · Bortable</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Swap</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        With <code>Swap="true"</code> the list no longer sorts. Instead, the item you drag over is highlighted, and
        on drop the dragged item trades places with it. Swapping works inside a single list and between lists that
        share a group. This mirrors the SortableJS
        <a href="https://github.com/SortableJS/Sortable/tree/master/plugins/Swap" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Swap plugin</a>.
    </p>
</div>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> A single list</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Nothing moves while you drag: the item under the pointer is highlighted with <code>SwapClass</code>, and the two
        trade places only when you drop.
    </p>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <SwapList />
    </div>

    <DemoCode For="typeof(SwapList)" />
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Across two lists</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Both lists have <code>Swap="true"</code> and the same <code>Group</code>: an item dropped onto an item of the
        other list trades places with it, and each list keeps its length.
    </p>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <SwapAcross />
    </div>

    <DemoCode For="typeof(SwapAcross)" />
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Try it</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>Drag <em>Item 1</em> onto <em>Item 5</em> and drop: the two trade places, and Items 2 to 4 stay where they were.</li>
        <li>Drag <em>Item 2</em> on the left onto <em>Item C</em> on the right: Item 2 goes right, Item C comes left.</li>
        <li>Drag an item over another and back over itself before dropping: no item is highlighted, and nothing changes.</li>
        <li>
            From the keyboard, pick an item up with <kbd>Space</kbd>: the arrows move the highlight instead of the item -
            "Item 1 will trade places with Item 3." - and <kbd>Space</kbd> makes the swap.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Good to know</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>
            Between lists, a swap needs each list to be able to take the other's item: the same group, a <code>Pull</code>
            that lets the item go, and a <code>Put</code> that lets it in.
        </li>
        <li>
            A swap brings one item into a list and takes one out, so a full list (<code>MaxItems</code>) never refuses it
            for room.
        </li>
        <li>
            A swap always trades the items themselves: a list whose <code>Pull</code> is <code>Clone</code> gives its item
            away in a swap rather than a copy of it.
        </li>
        <li>
            The highlighted item wears <code>SwapClass</code> - <code>bit-bortable-swap-highlight</code> by default, drawn
            with <code>--bit-bortable-swap-bg</code> and <code>--bit-bortable-swap-outline</code>. Pass a class of your own
            for a look of your own.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Parameters on this page</h3>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>Swap</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Trades places with the item it is dropped on instead of sorting.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>SwapClass</code></td><td><code>bit-bortable-swap-highlight</code></td><td>The class of the highlighted swap target.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>
